# What-IF settings. On Vercel, set these in Project → Settings → Environment Variables.
# Locally, set them in your shell before `npm run dev` or `npm run build`
#   Git Bash:   export WHATIF_BETA_CODES="BERLIN-CAFE-01,LINDEN-2026"
#   PowerShell: $env:WHATIF_BETA_CODES = "BERLIN-CAFE-01,LINDEN-2026"
# Never commit real invite codes; this file only shows the names.

# Closed beta invite codes, comma-separated, each at least 6 characters (case does not matter).
# Unset or empty = open (local development, tests). When set, every POST to /api/mcp needs a
# valid code: ?code=XXXX in the connector URL, an x-whatif-code header, or Authorization: Bearer XXXX.
# Read at runtime by the MCP function; on Vercel, a change applies from the next deployment.
# GET /api/mcp?code=XXXX answers { beta: { required, valid } } so the site can check a code.
WHATIF_BETA_CODES=

# Where owners ask for an invite code: an email address or a URL. Shown in the 401 message of the
# MCP endpoint and filled into the site and skill as {{BETA_CONTACT}} at build time.
# Empty = the site hides the "ask for a code" button.
WHATIF_BETA_CONTACT=

# The public site that report links, the skill zip and the Connect page point to, e.g.
# https://what-if.example.com (no trailing slash). Unset: a Vercel build uses
# https://$VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL, the MCP function uses the host of each request,
# and local builds use http://localhost:3000. The build warns when the skill zip would
# point at a localhost or non-https address.
WHATIF_SITE_URL=
